#=GF RN [2]
#=GF RM 12140321
#=GF RT Prediction of signal recognition particle RNA genes.
#=GF RA Regalia M, Rosenblad MA, Samuelsson T
#=GF RL Nucleic Acids Res 2002;30:3368-3377.
#=GF RN [3]
#=GF RM 19838050
#=GF RT Kinship in the SRP RNA family.
#=GF RA Rosenblad MA, Larsen N, Samuelsson T, Zwieb C
#=GF RL RNA Biol. 2009;6:508-516.
#=GF CC The signal recognition particle (SRP) is a universally conserved
#=GF CC ribonucleoprotein involved in the co-translational targeting of proteins
#=GF CC to membranes. The eukaryotic SRP consists of a 300-nucleotide 7S RNA
#=GF CC (RFAM:RF00017) and six proteins: SRPs 72, 68, 54, 19, 14, and 9. Archaeal
#=GF CC SRP consists of a 7S RNA and homologues of the eukaryotic SRP19 and SRP54
#=GF CC proteins. In most bacteria, the SRP consists of a 4.5S RNA and the Ffh
#=GF CC protein (a homologue of the eukaryotic SRP54 protein). Some Gram-positive
#=GF CC bacteria (e.g. Bacillus subtilis) have a longer eukaryote-like SRP RNA
#=GF CC that includes an Alu domain [2]. The Alu domain of the longer SRP RNAs is
#=GF CC not included in this model and therefore the reported bounds of some
#=GF CC members are incorrect. The Signal Recognition Particle Database (SRPDB)
#=GF CC [1] provides compilations of SRP components, with phylogenetic data and
#=GF CC structural illustrations.
#=GF WK Signal_recognition_particle_RNA
#=GF SQ 92

